is significant role in the history of logic, dialectic, and the genesis of thinking. In later intellectual contexts, Leibniz is said to have salvaged Aristotle by integrating form and matter and the powers into his own theory of substances and requisites, underscoring Aristotle’s enduring influence on metaphysical development. Aristotle is also depicted as representing an empirical, systematic approach to classification and knowledge in contrast to Plato’s division method. He elaborates on the nature of difference, contrariety, genus and species, mediation, and the idea of how differences may be inscribed within a concept, with attention to predication. He is described as the historical successor in the progression “from Aristotle onwards” in the development of representations, indicating his lasting position in the lineage of conceptual representation. Additionally, Aristotle is cited for distinguishing between difference and diversity and for explaining how genus, species, and differences relate in predication, further illustrating his role in shaping foundational distinctions used in philosophical and logical analysis.\",2\n73,BERGSON,\"BERGSON refers to Henri Bergson, a French philosopher (1859–1941) and the central figure of Bergsonism. His philosophy centers on durée (time lived as a qualitative flow), memory, consciousness, and the elan vital (a vital impetus) that drives creative evolution. Bergson distinguishes duration from ordinary clock time and from spatialized representations, arguing that time cannot be reduced to quantitative measurement. Memory is not mere passive recollection but a living dynamism that coexists with and continually reconstitutes the present. It comprises both active and passive syntheses—the reproduction of the recently past and the ongoing reflection of the new present—so that the past persists as memory-images or pure recollection and can be translated, rotated, contracted, or expanded within the current situation. This gives rise to the crystal-image, where actual and virtual images interpenetrate to mediate between perception and recollection and between the present and the past.\n\nBergson’s broader metaphysical program presents life as a creative differentiation that actualizes a virtual totality. He advances a monistic view that nonetheless accommodates novelty and differentiation, challenging purely mechanistic explanations of time and matter. The elan vital initiates and sustains progress beyond deterministic nature, enabling the emergence of new planes of nature and instruments of freedom through acts that realize what was only possible in the virtual. Time resists spatialization and is better understood as a plurality of rhythms and durations that unfold through continual variation and evolution. His approach to knowledge emphasizes intuition and the method of deriving understanding through duration, allowing genuine solutions to emerge and helping distinguish true problems from false ones by their formulation and resolution.\n\nKey concepts associated with Bergsonism include the idea that mind and memory are co-constitutive with consciousness, the open totality of time, and the critique of static abstractions such as rigid unity or fixed hierarchies. His influence extends to aesthetics and cinema, where memory, recollection, and the tension between actual and virtual images shape discussions of perception and representation. Notable works linked to his theories include Matter and Memory and Creative Evolution. In some Bergsonism discussions, Bergson is described as deemphasizing the story-telling aspect of art in favor of music’s emphasis on emotion and creation; the Bergsonian method is associated with intuition and the role of probability in forming knowledge. Overall, Bergson’s framework integrates memory, duration, and freedom within a dynamic, open conception of time and experience.\",12\n74,EINSTEIN,\"Albert Einstein (EINSTEIN) was a renowned 20th-century theoretical physicist best known for developing the theory of relativity, encompassing both special relativity and general relativity, and for pivotal contributions to quantum theory. His relativity framework revolutionized the understanding of space, time, and gravitation, while his work in quantum physics helped shape modern physics as a whole. In addition to these scientific achievements, Einstein engaged in notable philosophical exchanges about the nature of time and relativity, most famously in discussions with philosopher Henri Bergson. This dialogue highlighted the interplay between physics and philosophy and underscored debates about how scientific theories relate to human experience and perception. The discussions surrounding relativity and time are often framed within the mathematical context of Riemannian geometry, which provides the geometric language for general relativity and the description of spacetime structure. Through these contributions and dialogues, Einstein established a lasting scientific legacy that intertwines profound theoretical insights with interdisciplinary dialogue, reinforcing his status as a central figure in 20th-century science.\",2\n75,RIEMANN,\"Bernhard Riemann, 19th-century German mathematician whose work on Riemannian geometry underpins the concept of metric vs nonmetric multiplicities; his ideas are discussed in the text as modified by Bergson.\",2\n133,BERGSONISM,\"The Bergsonian philosophical framework or movement presented in the text, encompassing Bergson's ideas about perception, memory, and duration, and organizing the discussion around the two lines of experience and the role of intuition.\",2\n134,HAMELIN,\"Hamelin is a writer referenced by Bergson in the text; he is associated with an Essai sur les éléments principaux de la représentation (dated from 1907), which Bergson says he has in mind as part of his discussion on philosophy and representation.\",1\n135,TIME AND FREE WILL,Philosophical work referenced in the text; discusses durati