aced to the Phaedo, where recollection is tied to the ascent from perception to the grasp of eternal forms. The texts portray PLATO as articulating that ideas must be contemplated and concepts must be created, signaling his role in the origin of fundamental philosophical concepts and methods.\n\nPLATO is also presented as a master figure for later thinkers, including the neo-Platonists, whose lineage of concepts traces back to his dialogues and philosophical program. His method of division is cited as a historical precursor to later approaches to problem-solving and intuition, and his discussions in relation to the One and the Many (unity and multiplicity) are invoked to critique abstract, dialectical thinking, highlighting how representation and knowledge arise from a structured analysis of form and difference.\n\nIn relation to the sensible and the intelligible, PLATO is associated with discussions in the Republic and related dialogues (e.g., Philebus) about how the being of the sensible is constituted, the interplay of intensity and quality with sensibility, and how contrarieties presuppose an underlying identity of elements. Some descriptions tie him to claims that the divisible contains a nature in itself only by including the unequal, and to notions such as the instant atopon (an atemporal or place-less instant) within an Aion framework, used to explore temporality and eternity.\n\nAcross these descriptions, PLATO is consistently linked to memory, recollection, recognition, and the limits of perception and thought, underscoring his enduring role in shaping concepts of knowledge, reality, and the foundations of Western philosophy. He is portrayed as the progenitor of the theory of Forms, the origin of philosophical concepts, and a central influence on both ancient and later traditions of thought.\",6\n175,IMMANUEL KANT,\"IMMANUEL KANT is a German philosopher described in the text as the subject of a machinic portrait within the plane of immanence; the passage n